**You can keep all the files in one directory.** You do not need separate physical folders to process them concurrently.

The next change I recommend is:

> **One directory scanner → a bounded set of independent file-processing workers → one transactional Kafka producer per worker.**

Start by benchmarking **4 workers**, not thousands of threads. Each worker processes a different file while preserving the transactional checkpoint design.

## 1. Change the architecture—not just the parser

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    D["One directory<br/>thousands of CSV files"] --> S["One directory scanner"]
    S --> H["Stable filename-to-worker assignment"]
    H --> Q0["Bounded queue 0"]
    H --> Q1["Bounded queue 1"]
    H --> Q2["Bounded queue 2"]
    H --> Q3["Bounded queue 3"]

    Q0 --> W0["Worker 0<br/>Parser + producer"]
    Q1 --> W1["Worker 1<br/>Parser + producer"]
    Q2 --> W2["Worker 2<br/>Parser + producer"]
    Q3 --> W3["Worker 3<br/>Parser + producer"]

    W0 --> K["Kafka events"]
    W1 --> K
    W2 --> K
    W3 --> K

    W0 --> C0["Checkpoint topic 0"]
    W1 --> C1["Checkpoint topic 1"]
    W2 --> C2["Checkpoint topic 2"]
    W3 --> C3["Checkpoint topic 3"]
```

Each worker owns:

- Its CSV parser and current file.
- Its bounded JSON batch.
- Its Kafka producer.
- Its checkpoint index.
- Its stable transactional ID.
- Its dedicated, single-partition checkpoint topic.

**Do not share the previous `KafkaJournal` across workers.** Kafka producers are thread-safe, but each producer can have only one open transaction. Independent concurrent transactions require independent producers. ([kafka.apache.org](https://kafka.apache.org/41/javadoc/org/apache/kafka/clients/producer/KafkaProducer.html))

All workers can publish to the same output topic.

---

## 2. Keep file ownership stable

Assign files deterministically:

```java
static int workerFor(String fileName, int workerCount) {
    return Math.floorMod(fileName.hashCode(), workerCount);
}
```

For example:

```text
orders-001.csv → worker 2
orders-002.csv → worker 3
orders-003.csv → worker 0
```

This is a design choice that makes recovery straightforward: after restart, the same worker recovers the checkpoint for the same file.

Use identities such as:

```text
Worker 0:
  transactional.id = csv-orders-worker-0
  checkpoint topic = csv.checkpoints.orders.w0

Worker 1:
  transactional.id = csv-orders-worker-1
  checkpoint topic = csv.checkpoints.orders.w1
```

The transactional ID must remain stable for that logical worker across restarts. Kafka uses this identity for transaction recovery and fencing. ([kafka.apache.org](https://kafka.apache.org/41/javadoc/org/apache/kafka/clients/producer/KafkaProducer.html))

**Important:** with this simple assignment scheme, do not change `workerCount` after deployment without migrating checkpoints. Also keep the logical `sourceId` used in event IDs unchanged; worker identity and source identity are different things.

