## 5. Keep transactions batched

A starting configuration to benchmark:

```yaml
ingest:
  workers: 4
  queue-capacity: 32

  batch-rows: 10000
  batch-bytes: 8388608
  batch-max-age: 1s

  scan-delay: 2s
```

For every worker:

```text
Parse and serialize a bounded batch
                 ↓
Begin Kafka transaction
                 ↓
Send events asynchronously
                 ↓
Send checkpoint
                 ↓
Commit transaction
```

Do not call `.get()` after every `send()`. Kafka’s asynchronous sends permit batching; transaction commit completes outstanding sends. ([kafka.apache.org](https://kafka.apache.org/41/javadoc/org/apache/kafka/clients/producer/KafkaProducer.html))

Keep the previous producer tuning as a benchmark baseline:

```properties
batch.size=131072
linger.ms=10
compression.type=lz4
buffer.memory=67108864
```

**Memory now multiplies by worker count.** Four producers configured with 64 MiB buffers represent approximately 256 MiB of configured producer buffering alone, excluding JSON batches, parsing, compression, checkpoint maps, and other allocations. Kafka documents that `buffer.memory` is not a hard bound on total producer memory. ([kafka.apache.org](https://kafka.apache.org/41/configuration/producer-configs/))

